# sent_id = orat25.5.15
# text = Sein eifriges Naturstudium scheint ihn zu der Ansicht geführt zu haben, welche der Papst ihm vorwirft, der Mensch dürfe nur glauben, was durch Kraft und Grund der Natur (vi ac ratione naturae) könne bewiesen werden***); hatte er in seiner ersten Jugend, dem Zeitgeiste folgend, strenge Ketzergesetze erlassen, so machte ihn dagegen später sein Denken, seine Erfahrung, Umgang und Freundschaft mit edlen Männern eines fremden Bekenntnisses duldsam, und wo er ohne Rückhalt sich aussprach, erreichen seine Aeufserungen genau den Grad der Rücksichtlosigkeit, durch welchen König Friedrich Anstofs gegeben hat.
# reading_translation = His eager study of nature seems to have led him to the view that the pope reproaches him with, that man may believe only what can be proved by the force and reason of nature (vi ac ratione naturae)***); if in his early youth, following the spirit of the age, he had issued strict laws against heretics, yet later his thinking, his experience, and his intercourse and friendship with noble men of a different confession made him tolerant, and where he expressed himself without reserve, his utterances attain precisely the degree of disregard by which King Frederick gave offense.
